**Claim:** Yes, $f$ is locally injective on $M$.

**Proof.**

Let $k = \dim M$. The hypothesis says that for every $x \in M$ and every nonzero tangent vector $v \in T_x M$, we have $Df(x)\,v \neq 0$. Equivalently, the restricted linear map

$$Df(x)\big|_{T_x M} : T_x M \longrightarrow \mathbb{R}^n$$

has trivial kernel. Since $T_x M$ is finite-dimensional, trivial kernel means this restriction is **injective**. Hence the restriction $f|_M : M \to \mathbb{R}^n$ is an **immersion**.

We now apply the **local immersion theorem** (a standard consequence of the inverse function theorem). Fix $x \in M$. Because $Df(x)|_{T_x M}$ is injective, there exist

- an open neighborhood $V \subset M$ of $x$,
- an open neighborhood $W \subset \mathbb{R}^n$ of $f(x)$ with $f(V) \subset W$,
- local coordinates $(t_1,\dots,t_k)$ on $V$ centered at $x$, and
- a diffeomorphism $\phi : W \to \phi(W) \subset \mathbb{R}^n$ with $\phi(f(x)) = 0$,

such that, in these coordinates,

$$(\phi \circ f|_V)(t_1,\dots,t_k) \;=\; (t_1,\dots,t_k,0,\dots,0).$$

That is, $\phi \circ f|_V$ is the standard inclusion $\iota : \mathbb{R}^k \hookrightarrow \mathbb{R}^n$.

The standard inclusion $\iota$ is injective, and $\phi$ is a diffeomorphism (hence injective). Therefore

$$f|_V \;=\; \phi^{-1} \circ \iota \circ (\text{coordinate chart})$$

is a composition of injective maps, and consequently $f|_V$ is **injective**.

Since $x \in M$ was arbitrary, every point of $M$ admits an open neighborhood in $M$ on which $f$ is injective. By definition, $f$ is **locally injective on $M$**. $\blacksquare$

